{"causes":["The need for a standardized anatomical terminology arose from the diverse and inconsistent nomenclatures used in medical literature.","The global nature of medical practice required a unified language to facilitate communication among healthcare professionals from different countries.","Previous editions like the Nomina Anatomica highlighted the necessity for comprehensive and consistent anatomical terms."],"effects":["The Terminologia Anatomica became the definitive reference for anatomical terminology, influencing medical education worldwide.","It improved clarity and precision in anatomical communication, enhancing collaboration in research and clinical practice.","The standardization of terms has led to a more unified approach in anatomical studies and surgical practices globally."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"nomina-anatomica","sourceName":"Nomina Anatomica","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The Terminologia Anatomica was influenced by the earlier Nomina Anatomica, which highlighted the need for standardized anatomical terms."},{"sourceSlug":"federative-international-committee-on-anatomical-terminology","sourceName":"Federative International Committee on Anatomical Terminology","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The FICAT was responsible for creating the Terminologia Anatomica, establishing a global standard for anatomical terminology in 1998."},{"sourceSlug":"anatomy-conference-1998","sourceName":"Anatomy Conference 1998","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The formal presentation and endorsement of the Terminologia Anatomica occurred during the Anatomy Conference in 1998."},{"sourceSlug":"medical-education","sourceName":"Medical Education","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The Terminologia Anatomica defines the anatomical language used in medical education across various institutions."},{"sourceSlug":"surgical-practice","sourceName":"Surgical Practice","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The adoption of the Terminologia Anatomica has transformed surgical practice by providing clear and standardized anatomical terms."}],"places":[{"name":"Basel, Switzerland","role":"Publication site"},{"name":"London, United Kingdom","role":"Medical Education Hub"},{"name":"Berlin, Germany","role":"Anatomy Conference Location"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Terminologia Anatomica","type":"Reference article","slug":"terminologia-anatomica"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q286567","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminologia_Anatomica"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Terminologia Anatomica contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Terminologia Anatomica (TA) is the international standard of anatomical nomenclature — the authoritative list of standard anatomical terms for human gross anatomy — published by the Federative International Programme on Anatomical Terminologies (FIPAT, formerly FICAT) on behalf of the International Federation of Associations of Anatomists (IFAA). The current standard is Terminologia Anatomica 2 (TA2), published in 2019, superseding the first edition (TA1, 1998), which itself replaced the previous standard Nomina Anatomica (originally published 1895, last revised 1989). Terminologia Anatomica 2 contains approximately 7,500 terms designating structures of human gross anatomy in Latin (with English equivalents), organised hierarchically by body region and system.

Anatomical nomenclature — the systematic naming of anatomical structures — is one of the oldest branches of scientific terminology, with its origins in ancient Greek medicine (Galen, Herophilus) and its first systematic codification in the Renaissance anatomical tradition (Vesalius's De Humani Corporis Fabrica, 1543). The Basel Nomina Anatomica (BNA, 1895) was the first internationally agreed standard, produced by a commission of 50 anatomists at the Anatomische Gesellschaft meeting in Basel — replacing a chaos of competing national and personal naming traditions with a single Latin standard. The subsequent international revision processes (the Jena Nomina Anatomica 1935, the Paris Nomina Anatomica 1955, the revisions of 1961, 1966, 1977, 1983, and 1989, and the Terminologia Anatomica 1998 and 2019) represent a century-long process of international scientific collaboration to standardise the language of anatomy.

Terminologia Anatomica matters both as a practical scientific standard (enabling communication across languages in anatomy, surgery, radiology, and clinical medicine) and as a symbol of the internationalisation of science — the aspiration to replace the diversity of national terminological traditions with a single universal language of anatomy, accessible to medical practitioners worldwide.","subjects":["Medical Science","Modern Era","Anatomy","Scientific Nomenclature","Medical Education","Latin","International Standards","Clinical Medicine"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Terminologia Anatomica reshaped the communication of anatomical knowledge, significantly impacting medical education and practice globally, leading to better patient care and research collaboration.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
